---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const title = "Luca Sanna — Biography and Artistic Practice";
const description =
    "Biography and artistic practice of Luca Sanna, Italian visual artist based in Sardinia, working across painting, drawing and digital image-making.";
---

<BaseLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    canonicalUrl="https://lucasanna.art/en/biography/"
    context="page"
    locale="en"
>
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-0 pb-12 md:pt-2">
        <header class="mb-6 md:mb-7 text-center">
            <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-heading font-normal mb-6 leading-tight text-white">
                Biography
            </h1>
        </header>

        <div class="mb-12">
            <img
                src="/assets/uploads/Tramonto-con-luna-piena-acrilico-su-tela-2023.webp"
                alt="Artwork by Luca Sanna"
                class="w-full h-auto object-cover"
            />
        </div>

        <div class="prose prose-invert prose-lg mx-auto text-gray-300 leading-relaxed font-light tracking-wide">
            <h2>About Luca Sanna</h2>
            <p>
                I am <strong>Luca Sanna</strong>, an Italian visual artist based in Sardinia. My practice grows out of a deep relationship with the landscapes of the island: the sea, rock, roots and the changing light that moves across them.
            </p>
            <p>
                In my paintings and drawings, landscape is never merely a setting. It becomes body, memory and vision. At times it remains empty and silent; elsewhere it receives figures and faces that merge with rocks, trees or water, creating a suspended space between reality and dream.
            </p>
            <p>
                Roots and branches intertwine like veins of the earth. Trees become watchful presences; eyes and faces emerge from and dissolve into the landscape, observing while being observed.
            </p>
            <p>
                Reflections of moonlight and sunlight on water — often at night, when the sea becomes a mirror — open visionary passages, while birds appear in the distance or merge into the background within intense, natural colour fields.
            </p>
            <p>
                For me, colour does not simply describe: <strong>it evokes</strong>. It is both symbolic and emotional. Blues and greens suggest balance and quiet; ochres and earth tones recall roots and memory; sharper contrasts open more surreal and interior spaces.
            </p>

            <h2>Artistic practice</h2>
            <p>
                My work develops along three recurring axes: <strong>metamorphosis</strong>, where figures and landscapes transform into one another; <strong>symbiosis</strong>, the inseparable relationship between human presence and nature; and the <strong>oneiric</strong>, a dreamlike dimension that allows the image to move beyond the boundaries of the visible.
            </p>
            <p>
                Sardinian landscapes are a point of departure, not a boundary. Each work seeks a broader visual language in which the visible meets the invisible and silence becomes an active presence.
            </p>

            <h2>Recurring themes</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Empty landscapes:</strong> spaces of silence and contemplation without human figures.</li>
                <li><strong>Faces merging with landscape:</strong> presences emerging from rock, trees and roots.</li>
                <li><strong>Roots and branches:</strong> organic lines associated with memory, continuity and belonging.</li>
                <li><strong>Eyes within nature:</strong> concealed gazes among trees and rocks, suggesting a diffuse consciousness.</li>
                <li><strong>Lunar and solar reflections:</strong> light on water, often nocturnal, opening toward interior visions.</li>
                <li><strong>Birds and animal presences:</strong> vital, symbolic elements within the chromatic space.</li>
                <li><strong>Symbolic colour:</strong> emotional and visionary palettes that translate memories and states of mind.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Media and materials</h2>
            <p>
                I work with <strong>acrylic, watercolour and pencil</strong>, choosing each medium according to the needs of the image. Acrylic allows layering and contrast; watercolour offers vibration and transparency; pencil gives precision to the line and to processes of transformation.
            </p>

            <h2>Background</h2>
            <p>
                I was born in Sassari in October 1973. After studying at the Academy of Fine Arts in Sassari, I developed a practice that connects landscape, nature and surreal imagery. I live and work in Sardinia, where painting and drawing remain central while digital tools enter the process when they offer a meaningful extension of the image.
            </p>
            <p>
                Over time I have also built an online archive of this practice, keeping the focus on the works, the writing around them and the dialogue with those who follow the research.
            </p>

            <h2>Follow the work</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><a href="https://www.instagram.com/lucasanna_art">Instagram</a></li>
                <li><a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@lucasannart">TikTok</a></li>
                <li><a href="https://www.facebook.com/lucasannadesign">Facebook</a></li>
                <li><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@LucaSanna">YouTube</a></li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                For exhibitions, collaborations or commissions: <a href="mailto:info@lucasanna.art">info@lucasanna.art</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
                You can explore the <a href="/en/works/">works</a> or <a href="/en/contact/">get in touch</a> directly.
            </p>
        </div>
    </article>
</BaseLayout>
